Chapter: LirTargets
  cites Codex chapter Lir (LirTarget)

 Register-file descriptors that belong to no selector.

 The shipping descriptor, `lir-target-x86`, deliberately does not live
 here: it sits beside `lir-phys` in `Emit/X86_64Lir.codex` because
 `nregs` is the length of that mapping, and two files cannot be trusted
 to keep that true. A descriptor with a physical mapping belongs next to
 its mapping.

 A descriptor with NO mapping has nowhere else to go, and that is what
 this chapter is for. `IR/Lir.codex` states that it names no machine and
 the allocator should keep that property, so the probe cannot go there
 either.

Section: The ARM64-Shaped Probe

 The register file is "x86-64-shaped first", and ARM64 being a table
 entry is a design intent, not a proven fact until the retarget is
 done. The retarget's first step made the file data; this is the
 second, which is the cheapest available test of whether that
 was enough.

 It is a SHAPE, not a machine. There is no `lir-phys` for it, nothing
 emits from it, and no ARM64 instruction is selected anywhere on its
 path. Naming it `a64-shape` rather than `a64` is deliberate: a real
 ARM64 descriptor arrives in a later retarget step with a mapping
 beside it, and the two must not be confused for one another.

 What it varies, and why each one is the interesting axis:

 **More registers.** Ten callee-saved against x86's four and eight
 argument registers against four, so the file is 19 wide where x86 is 9.
 x86-64 sits at the register count that is linear scan's
 hard case; a wider file exercises the arithmetic in the other
 direction, where the free-set walks and the occupant array are longer
 than anything the shipping target has ever handed them.

 **A different callee/arg split.** The pools are not merely bigger, they
 are cut in a different place. `[0, ncallee)` is callee-saved,
 `ncallee` alone is the scratch return register, and `[nargregs, nregs)`
 is the argument file -- so a helper that happened to be right because
 `nargregs` was `ncallee + 1` at four and five is right here for the
 same reason at ten and eleven, and one that had folded the two
 together is not.

 **No division-fixed pair.** This is the axis with a known trap behind
 it. ARM64 `sdiv` and RISC-V `div` are three-operand and fix nothing, so
 both indices are -1, and every consumer of the pair has to mean it: a
 bare `list-set-at occ (0 - 1)` is an out-of-range write, so a target
 with no fixed pair would corrupt the verifier's occupant map rather
 than exclude nothing. `Lir.codex` claims each of those sites is
 guarded. This descriptor is what asks.

  lir-target-a64-shape : LirTarget = LirTarget {
   ncallee = 10,
   nargregs = 11,
   nregs = 19,
   diva = 0 - 1,
   divb = 0 - 1,
   budget = 24
  }

Section: The Narrow Probe

 A wide file tests LESS than a narrow one, and the first run of the
 descriptor above is what showed it: with nineteen registers not one
 definition in the `lir-check` corpus spilled, so the crossing arm, the
 Belady eviction and the whole spill path never ran. The corpus was built
 to reach x86-64's pressure points at nine registers, and at nineteen it
 is all slack. A green from the wide probe alone would have been a green
 over paths nothing executed.

 So the second descriptor keeps the character that matters -- no
 division-fixed pair, a split that is not x86's -- and makes the file
 narrow enough to force the allocator into the corners: two callee-saved
 registers, one scratch return, two argument registers.

 This is the combination x86-64 can never present, and it is the one
 worth asking about: every spill and eviction the shipping target has
 ever taken was taken with a division-fixed pair to mask. Here the mask
 is empty and the file is full at the same time.

  lir-target-narrow-shape : LirTarget = LirTarget {
   ncallee = 2,
   nargregs = 3,
   nregs = 5,
   diva = 0 - 1,
   divb = 0 - 1,
   budget = 8
  }
